l array and i  shift it by 20 well that's going to  shift each date by 20 nanoseconds and  notice that the type of the array has  changed to be a nanosecond  representation and that's the same thing  i show here where i have dates and i  combine them with nanoseconds we get a  nanosecond array  what about missing time uh well the date  time and date objects do not offer  specialized objects for missing time we  just would use none to serve as a  placeholder whereas with datetime 64 we  have nat not a time which is a nan like  value that can live in date time 64  arrays  so  those nat values come from nuns or empty  strings in the creation of a daytime 64  array so here i create an array i say i  want daytime 64 dates and i provide none  as my value this is going to get  converted to an nat the same thing  happens if i provide an empty string  and nats propagate just like nands under  transformation so if i have an array  with an nat and i perform a time delta  on it  that is going to propagate the nat in  the expected position  okay what about the performance of these  different types of  representations  so  you might think why can't we just put  date time objects in object arrays um  well object arrays uh in order for  daytime objects to live in object arrays  numpy's going to have to unbox each of  those objects and perform operations on  them that's going to be considerably  slower than what we can do with  contiguous data when we have date time  64 arrays what we have are c arrays with  contiguous bytes and that's always going  to give us much better performance  so i'm going to do some performance  comparisons you can find these in the  static frame repository if you want to  look at these or run them yourself we're  going to experiment with about 11 000  dates and i've created two arrays a  datetime 64 array and an object array of  date time objects and if we look at the  performance of these guys we see that  daytime 64 is is always going to be  faster so if