nd units convert to offset  integers both of those may not be what  you expect so here we have a numpy array  of date time 64 days and we're going to  ask type these to object and we see that  numpy nicely gives us back date objects  and nothing is lost  we can do the same thing by going to  list but if we have date time 64 months  and we convert those to objects we're  actually going to get date objects we're  going to introduce a day here that we  didn't have before and the same thing  happens with years so that's something  to watch out for  um  if we want to try to convert a  nanosecond or anything lower than  microsecond to object uh we're going to  see that numpy is going to give us back  integers these are the raw offsets um  and this is likely to be an undesirable  situation  going from daytime to date time 64 is  quite straightforward here all we need  to do is take our values and as type  them to the appropriate datetime 64  representation  okay so what we often do with our date  times is we're using them to label data  and we can do this in pandas and we can  do this in static frame  and pandas and static frame take very  different approaches here so pandas  provides one datetime index type the  datetime index and it forces all  datetime 64s to become nanosex  nanoseconds regardless of  what you ask for staticframe has one  index type per daytime 64 unit  and we can use datetime64 units to  explicitly specify our resolution and  our type  so in pandas every datetime 64 is a  nanosecond pandas stores all values as  nanosecond daytime 64 arrays you don't  have a choice any daytime 64 array given  as an index or given as values is  converted to nanoseconds without without  asking you uh we also have this  pd timestamp object which allows a  nanosecond resolution in the object  domain  so  why use nanoseconds everywhere a few of  us use data at the nanosecond resolution  and using nanosecond resolution  compromises the range of more common  units like dates  and using nanosecond resolution forces  the creation of this pd.timestamp object  and again we have an option to align  with python's microsecond unit in its  own date time but that option was not  taken  so let me show you an example of this so  if we have if we create a series here  with a date time index and here i'm  specifically giving date objects i'm  saying the resolution of my data is at  the level of date we can create a series  this way and things look fine when we  just look at the series but if we look  at the underlying array in the index we  can see that it's been converted to  nanoseconds  um and if you're a reminder you have  about two minutes left okay thank you  and if we convert this to object uh we  see we get a timestamp object um we can  as type this at the level of the array  and we get the expected integer offsets  um and it doesn't matter how we put the  nanosecond array in the pandas container  if we put the array in as a raw index  pandas is going to convert it to a  nanosecond fluoresce if we put it in as  the actual values pandas is also going  to convert it to a nanosecond  with static frame we've taken a very  different approach we have an index type  per unit so we have a family of index  types this encourages the specification  of a resolution it allows us to take  full advantage of the available range  and we don't have a need for pd  timestamp so static frame offers this  full family of index types one per unit  and when we create  containers when we create labels we can  specify the intended resolution so if i  have some monthly data i can create an  index year month and that information  that resolution is retained and we can  see under the hood we have a daytime 64  month array uh backing this data  if i want to represent the dates of the  magna carta or when martin luther posted  his 95 theses i can do that and i can  create an index date object with dates  in 12 15 and 15 17. this cannot be  represented in pandas and if i try